WEBVTT

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:14.000
The pounding surf of the great southern ocean beating on the rocks of South Georgia.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.640
Few creatures you might think could survive it, but macaroni penguins are desperate to

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:36.080
get ashore.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.040
Their flippers are of little help out of water.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:57.160
All they have to give them a grip on these slippery rocks are the small claws on their

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:02.880
feet.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.600
Now at the end of summer, life is becoming increasingly difficult for these macaroni

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:13.920
penguins, struggling to feed their chicks that are almost fully grown and have massive

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.720
aquatides.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:21.560
With the approach of autumn, the weather will worth massive depressions rush around the

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:27.640
fringes of the Antarctic continent, creating huge gales that gust to over 100 miles an

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.240
hour and lash the sea into a frenzy.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:36.200
Before long, the temperatures will drop to below freezing and then all the wildlife of

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:42.520
Antarctica will be engaged in a desperate race to complete breeding before the ice closes

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:49.040
everything down.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.320
In the deep south, the sea has stayed frozen all summer.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:03:00.280
The penguins here face an even greater challenge, for this is where the door closes first.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:07.480
Here at Cape Roids, I'm 1,400 miles closer to the pole, and this, a daily colony, is

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:12.080
the most southerly nesting group of any penguins anywhere.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.080
The summer here is very short indeed, and these penguins have to breed very swiftly

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.040
if they're to be successful.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.960
They're well ahead of the macaronis up in the north, and the chicks are already losing

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.680
their down.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.640
Beneath the woolly coat lies the waterproof layer of feathers that will protect them in

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:42.720
the icy southern seas.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.800
The season is so short that things have to move fast.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.620
Over a mere two weeks, the jam-packed colony virtually empties as the newly feathered young

00:03:52.620 --> 00:04:10.880
follow their parents down to the sea to make their first encounter with water.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:14.000
And their first swim will not be easy.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.040
The bay is filled with surging, sharp-edged brash ice.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:27.840
Even getting down to the water poses problems.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.840
Soon the edge of the sea is thronged by apprehensive youngsters, nervously waiting for someone

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:39.640
to take the plunge.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:45.160
The brash is so thick and extensive that on its seaward side, adults returning with food

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:59.520
for their chicks can't get through.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:08.960
They turn back.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:12.080
The hungry youngsters now have little alternative.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.120
They have to get to sea to feed.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:26.160
In fact, it's easier for them to cross the brash than for their parents.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.800
Being significantly lighter and more buoyant, they can skitter across the surface of the

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:43.080
broken ice.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:53.440
But moving so slowly and so clumsily puts them in real danger.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:06:13.360
A leopard seal.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.560
The majority of the chicks make it to open water, where they're a little safer.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:37.560
The leopard seal stays with its victim.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:58.680
And by midday, the existedş

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:21.280
This game of cat and mouse goes on for 20 minutes.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.420
Like so many other large predators on land and on sea, the leopard seal seems to feel

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:30.160
no urgency to complete its kill.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:39.240
At last, the penguin is dead.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:08:04.240
Now the process of stripping off its flesh begins.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:29.440
The carcass drifts down to the sea floor, but it won't be wasted.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:43.960
A nematine worm, a metre long, it has detected the taste of penguin flesh drifting through

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:56.200
the cold water.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:02.200
Another scavenger arrives, a giant isopod, 10 centimetres long, the equivalent of crabs

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:12.800
in warmer waters.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:17.560
The isopod strips off the meat with its hooked legs and strong jaws.

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:25.080
The worm just turns its stomach inside out and envelops the food.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:33.640
Within hours, the carcass is covered by a writhing tangle of worms.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:42.000
Within days, there is nothing left but bare bones.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:46.720
The first snows of winter have fallen.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:49.400
The last chicks to hatch are doomed.

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:54.120
Their parents have to abandon them before they're fully grown.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:59.120
The adults must go to sea to build up their strength before returning to the colony for

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:04.200
one last ordeal before winter sets in, the molt.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:09.160
All penguins need a new coat of feathers for the winter, and that means shedding the old

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:10.160
one.

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:20.320
And colonies right around the continent fill with shed feathers.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:32.120
On Deception Island, chinstrap penguins stand silent and motionless.

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:38.640
Only a month ago, these steep slopes of volcanic ash were noisy with the squawks of 80,000

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:44.120
pairs of them coming and going and caring for their chicks.

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:47.280
Now the penguins have little energy to spare.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:58.440
They can't go to sea with their coats in this condition, so they can't feed.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:03.240
For three weeks, they stand fasting, losing half their body weight.

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:16.880
But at the end, they will have warm, watertight coats and be ready for the icy blasts of winter.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:22.360
By the end of March, most of them have left, and the remainder are on the move, making

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:50.360
their way across the emptying slopes back to the sea.

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:57.400
To the north, to open seas, is the driving force, to move where the food should be.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:25.160
But the obstacles are formidable.

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:31.440
At minus 1.9 degrees centigrade, the sea begins to freeze.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:41.040
A slight swell on the surface produces pancake ice.

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:47.280
In the frigid air, the ice above water grows into crystals.

00:12:47.280 --> 00:13:01.660
The early explorers called these fantastic shapes ice flowers.

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:05.600
As it gets colder and colder, the ice thickens.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:11.880
On the coast, it freezes fast to the margins of the land.

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:18.280
Over out, the pack ice consolidates into sea ice.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:24.000
The belt of ice surrounding the continent widens, advancing northwards two miles a day

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:29.400
and driving life before it.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:33.600
But the ice front has not yet reached all the islands, and there are still some that

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:46.440
can provide a refuge for wildlife well into autumn.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:51.560
Here on South Georgia, we're on the northern edge of Antarctica.

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:55.120
You can be fairly sure that the sea here won't freeze over.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:58.060
Only once or twice in a century does it do so.

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:02.360
This floating ice has all fallen from the glacier behind me.

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:08.480
But although at 54 degrees south, we're as far away from the South Pole as Britain is

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:17.880
from the north, the immense ice cap of Antarctica still dominates the climate.

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:20.300
Glaciers cover over half the island.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:26.440
They blanket many of the peaks, the tallest of which are 2,700 metres high, and in some

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:30.760
places they run right down into the sea.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:35.560
During the winter, the temperature falls to minus 10 degrees at the coast, so the need

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:43.040
for animals to complete their breeding in the short summer season is still very intense.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:50.040
Two million fur seals come here to breed, and at the end of summer, the beaches are

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:56.020
thronged with young pups and their mothers.

00:14:56.020 --> 00:14:59.960
The pups suckle for four months until late March.

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:03.880
It's longer than the fur seals that live in warmer waters farther north.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:11.360
It's a measure of how strong young animals have to be if they're to survive down here.

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:22.200
A pup, if it is to get all the milk that is its due, has to recognise its mother's call

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:45.320
when she returns from feeding at sea and is ready to provide a feed.

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:50.360
Three months earlier, this shore was a battlefield as the bulls fought for the right to dominate

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:53.920
a stretch of beach and all the females on it.

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:57.240
Now the mating is finished and the bulls have gone to sea.

00:15:57.240 --> 00:16:21.120
Here the pups are left, testing their strength with mock fights.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:41.300
But one of them might lose their

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:57.740
Many of the youngsters playing here will not survive their first year.

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:00.180
The weaker ones will not get enough food.

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:01.180
There will be accidents.

00:17:01.180 --> 00:17:04.060
There will be orphans.

00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:09.060
By the end of the breeding season, corpses lie scattered over the beach, food for the

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:11.060
skewers and the giant petrels.

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:50.380
The petrels, with their great hooked beaks, are usually the first to rip open a carcass.

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:54.020
They are the Antarctic's equivalent of Africa's vultures.

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:57.060
Their huge wings are two metres across.

00:17:57.060 --> 00:18:00.140
But unlike vultures, they don't just scavenge.

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:24.020
They will tackle young penguins and small seabirds while they're still alive.

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:40.180
The whalers in the old days used to call them gluttonies.

00:18:40.180 --> 00:18:41.180
It's easy to see why.

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:48.580
And their dirtiness gave them another name too, stinkers.

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:51.860
Surprisingly there are ducks at this feast too.

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:54.220
These are the South Georgia pintails.

00:18:54.220 --> 00:19:22.580
Alone among ducks, they've acquired a regular taste for meat.

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:39.980
An elephant seal wallow.

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:42.100
This is an all-female gathering.

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:46.060
They clearly like one another's company, for they congregate in great assemblies.

00:19:46.060 --> 00:20:02.340
But that doesn't mean that they don't on occasion get irritated with one another.

00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:07.300
Like the penguins, they went to sea after rearing their young, fed intensively to put

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:17.460
on the weight they lost during breeding, and now they've come back in order to molt.

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:26.620
Large chunks of skin and hair peel off their bodies, and it seems to make them very catchy.

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:29.780
It takes a month for them to grow new coats.

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:34.500
Then as the temperature falls still lower and winter closes in, they will return to

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:43.580
the place where they're most at home, the sea.

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:53.260
Grey-headed albatross also nest on South Georgia, but they stay a little longer.

00:20:53.260 --> 00:21:18.900
The waters are still ice-free, so they can catch food for their young well into autumn.

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:25.340
An adult bird, caring for its chick, may travel 600 miles or more to find food, which it brings

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:39.420
back in its crop.

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:45.940
That was a squid, and very nice too.

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:51.860
Of the grey-heads, another kind of albatross, the largest seabird in the world with a three-meter

00:21:51.860 --> 00:21:56.780
wingspan, the wandering albatross.

00:21:56.780 --> 00:22:10.020
It nests a little further inland on South Georgia's meadows and ridges of tussock grass.

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:14.260
In marked contrast to the other birds that have almost finished their breeding and are

00:22:14.260 --> 00:22:20.060
preparing to leave, this wandering albatross has come to start a courtship that may take

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:35.220
two or three years.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:40.260
These young birds have spent the first three years of their adult life at sea.

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:44.420
Now they've returned to the colony where they were reared and are starting to look for a

00:22:44.420 --> 00:23:11.420
partner, and the way they do this is by taking part in dancing parties.

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:23.500
Young, unmated birds court like this for several years before they decide who their partners

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:41.940
shall be and together start work on a nest man.

00:23:41.940 --> 00:24:01.420
But as the winter sets in and its icy door closes, the young albatross too have to return

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:21.660
to the colony.

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:28.620
The sea won't freeze here around South Georgia, but as the sun moves north and the days darken,

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:33.980
the temperature of the ocean falls lower still and life in the water becomes increasingly

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:35.860
scarce.

00:24:35.860 --> 00:24:41.700
The huge shoals of krill disperse and for the sea birds food becomes more and more difficult

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:53.000
to find.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:25:09.220
By April, winter storms are beginning to sweep across the Antarctic.

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:27.700
The winds rise to above 100 miles an hour.

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:36.300
The temperature falls to 70 degrees below zero.

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:39.220
And then the sea freezes.

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:44.740
The door has shut.

00:25:44.740 --> 00:25:48.740
Throughout the winter, the ice continues to advance northwards.

00:25:48.740 --> 00:25:55.340
The area it covers increases at the rate of 40,000 square miles every day.

00:25:55.340 --> 00:26:17.100
Before the winter is over, it will have almost doubled the size of the continent.

00:26:17.100 --> 00:26:38.900
Now, at the end of autumn, practically all the wildlife has escaped to the north.

00:26:38.900 --> 00:26:42.660
The whales have gone to find warmer waters in which to breed.

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:47.700
The seals, the albatrosses and most of the penguins have also gone out to sea, though

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:51.060
no one as yet is sure exactly where.

00:26:51.060 --> 00:26:56.060
But there is one truly remarkable creature that seems to turn all these rules upside

00:26:56.060 --> 00:26:57.340
down.

00:26:57.340 --> 00:27:01.620
The emperor penguin.

00:27:01.620 --> 00:27:07.780
Largest of all the penguins, the emperor stands over a meter high and weighs on average 33

00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:09.180
kilos.

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:13.660
These creatures are forced by the worsening weather to retreat north to warmer latitudes.

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:19.900
But the emperors are gathering at the ice edge to start traveling into the deep south

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:43.780
where they will mate and rear their young.

00:27:43.780 --> 00:28:13.340
Now, the emperors start their long march, maybe tens of miles, to reach their traditional

00:28:13.340 --> 00:28:35.780
nesting site on the sea ice.

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:51.780
Hey, baby!
